Task 1: Managing VM Lifecycles with Command-Line Tools
Subtask 1.1: Install and Configure virtctl
First, ensure you have the necessary command-line tools installed and configured.

Verify oc client installation:
oc version
Install virtctl if not already available:
# Download virtctl (replace with current version)
curl -L -o virtctl https://github.com/kubevirt/kubevirt/releases/download/v0.59.0/virtctl-v0.59.0-linux-amd64
chmod +x virtctl
sudo mv virtctl /usr/local/bin/
Verify virtctl installation:
virtctl version
Login to your OpenShift cluster:
oc login -u <username> -p <password> <cluster-url>
Subtask 1.2: Create a Sample Virtual Machine
Before managing VM lifecycles, create a test virtual machine.

Create a new project for VM testing:
oc new-project vm-lifecycle-lab
Create a sample VM YAML file:
cat > sample-vm.yaml << EOF
apiVersion: kubevirt.io/v1
kind: VirtualMachine
metadata:
  name: test-vm
  namespace: vm-lifecycle-lab
spec:
  running: false
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        kubevirt.io/vm: test-vm
    spec:
      domain:
        cpu:
          cores: 1
        devices:
          disks:
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: containerdisk
          - disk:
              bus: virtio
            name: cloudinitdisk
          interfaces:
          - masquerade: {}
            name: default
        machine:
          type: pc-q35-rhel8.4.0
        resources:
          requests:
            memory: 1Gi
      networks:
      - name: default
        pod: {}
      volumes:
      - containerDisk:
          image: quay.io/kubevirt/cirros-container-disk-demo
        name: containerdisk
      - cloudInitNoCloud:
          userData: |
            #cloud-config
            password: fedora
            chpasswd: { expire: False }
        name: cloudinitdisk
EOF
Apply the VM configuration:
oc apply -f sample-vm.yaml
Verify VM creation:
oc get vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
Subtask 1.3: Start and Stop VMs using virtctl
Now practice starting and stopping VMs using the virtctl command.

Start the virtual machine:
virtctl start test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
Check VM status:
oc get vm test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
oc get vmi test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
Monitor VM startup process:
virtctl console test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
# Press Ctrl+] to exit console
Stop the virtual machine:
virtctl stop test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
Verify VM has stopped:
oc get vm test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
oc get vmi test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
Subtask 1.4: Restart and Pause Operations
Learn additional lifecycle operations for comprehensive VM management.

Start the VM again:
virtctl start test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
Restart the running VM:
virtctl restart test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
Pause the virtual machine:
virtctl pause vmi test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
Check paused status:
oc get vmi test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab -o yaml | grep phase
Unpause the virtual machine:
virtctl unpause vmi test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab
Subtask 1.5: Using oc Commands for VM Management
Practice using native OpenShift commands for VM lifecycle management.

Start VM using oc patch:
oc patch vm test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":true}}'
Stop VM using oc patch:
oc patch vm test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab --type merge -p '{"spec":{"running":false}}'
Check VM specification:
oc get vm test-vm -n vm-lifecycle-lab -o yaml | grep running
